course: everyday
lesson: 234
title: Snow, ice and strong sun
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Cold is most dangerous when you are wet. Wet clothes lose heat fast, so fingers and toes can get frostbite and the whole body can get too cold. Shivering hard, clumsiness and sleepiness are signs of hypothermia, and they mean «get warm and dry now».
explain: Snow and ice also hide dangers from above and below: heavy snow and icicles can fall from roofs, snow piles can collapse, ice is slippery, and snowploughs push snow off the road into the piles by the kerb.
explain: Strong sun is a danger too. The sun is strongest from about 11 a.m. to 3 p.m., and in Australia and New Zealand the UV is especially strong. Glass can focus sunlight into a spot hot enough to start a fire.
explain: Example: a boy in Calgary plays in the snow until his gloves are soaked. His fingers go white and numb, so he goes in, takes off the wet gloves and warms his hands slowly, and tells his mum.
fact: In snow, wear a hat, gloves, warm socks and waterproof boots, and come in when you get wet or very cold.
fact: Wet clothes lose heat fast, and fingers and toes can get frostbite.
fact: Shivering hard and feeling clumsy and sleepy in the cold are signs of hypothermia, so tell a grown-up and get warm and dry.
fact: Throw only soft snowballs at people who are playing, never at faces, cars or strangers, and never with stones or ice inside; a snowball can startle a driver into a crash.
fact: Keep snow forts small and open, and never dig tunnels in snow piles by the road, because snow collapses and snowploughs push snow there.
fact: Never stand under icicles or knock them down, because they are heavy and sharp.
fact: Do not eat yellow, grey or dirty snow, and ask before eating any, because snow can contain dirt, animal pee and road salt.
fact: Walk around frozen puddles, because ice is very slippery.
fact: Keep away from under roof edges after heavy snow, because snow can slide off and bury or hurt someone.
fact: On an outdoor rink, skate in the same direction as everyone else and get up quickly after a fall, because skate blades can cut hands.
fact: When the UV index is high, stay in the shade from about 11 a.m. to 3 p.m., when the sun is strongest.
fact: If you get sunburnt, get out of the sun, cool the skin with cool water, drink and tell a grown-up.
fact: A magnifying glass focuses sunlight into a spot hot enough to start a fire, so never point it at anything, at people or at animals.
fact: Pick up glass bottles from dry grass, because a bottle can focus sunlight and start a grass fire.
exercise: What should you wear to play in the snow? | A hat, gloves, warm socks and waterproof boots | Trainers and a thin jacket | Just a big coat, nothing else matters | Cotton gloves that dry quickly
exercise: Your gloves and socks are soaked from the snow. What should you do? | Go in and change into dry things | Keep playing in them until they dry out | Wring them out and put them back on | Take them off and play bare-handed
exercise: Why are wet clothes in the cold dangerous? | They lose heat fast and can lead to frostbite | They freeze stiff into a kind of armour that keeps you warm | They are only uncomfortable, never dangerous | They make the snow stick to you so you get heavier
exercise: Your friend is shivering hard, stumbling and says he is sleepy. What should you do? | Tell a grown-up at once and get him warm and dry | Let him lie down for a little nap in the soft snow | Tell him to run around by himself until he warms up | Give him snow to eat for energy
exercise: What are hard shivering, clumsiness and sleepiness in the cold signs of? | Hypothermia, when the body is too cold | Just being tired after a long morning of playing | Having eaten too little breakfast before going out | Sunburn from the glare off the white snow
exercise: Which snowball is fair to throw? | A soft one at a friend who is playing | A hard icy one at a friend's back | Any snowball at all at a passing car | A soft one at the face of a stranger walking by
exercise: Why should you never put a stone in a snowball? | It can badly hurt someone's eye or face | It makes the snowball melt faster | It is cheating in a snowball fight | It makes it too heavy to throw
exercise: You want to dig a tunnel in the big snow pile by the road. Why is that dangerous? | It can collapse, and snowploughs push more snow onto it | The snow by the road is far too dirty and salty to dig in | Cars need that snow to help them stop | The tunnel will melt away before you have time to finish it
exercise: Long icicles hang from the roof over the door. What should you do? | Not stand under them or knock them down | Knock them all off the roof with a long stick | Stand right under them so you can catch one as it falls | Break one off to suck on like an ice lolly
exercise: Is it okay to eat yellow snow? | No, it may contain animal pee | Yes, it is just snow with sunlight on it | Yes, if it is fresh | Only if it is very cold
exercise: Why should you ask before eating any snow by the road? | It can contain dirt and road salt | Road snow is always warmer | It melts too fast | It belongs to the council
exercise: There is a frozen puddle on the way to school. What should you do? | Walk around it | Slide across it fast | Jump on it to crack it | Run over it in trainers
exercise: After heavy snow, why should you not stand right under the edge of a roof? | Snow can slide off and bury or hurt you | The roof will drip cold water all over your hat | Snow on roofs is always warmer | Birds like to sit up there in winter and may peck at you
exercise: You fall over on an outdoor ice rink. What should you do? | Get up quickly so skates do not cut your hands | Lie still on the ice until someone comes to help | Crawl slowly along the ice all the way to the side | Stay sitting and rest
exercise: Why should you skate the same way as everyone else? | So you do not crash into others | Ice freezes one way only | You go faster the other way | It is only a rule for adults
exercise: The UV index is high. When should you stay in the shade? | About 11 a.m. to 3 p.m., when the sun is strongest | Only early in the morning, before breakfast | Only after 5 p.m. | Only when it is cloudy, because clouds make the sun stronger
exercise: Your shoulders are red and sore after a day outside. What should you do? | Get out of the sun, cool the skin, drink and tell a grown-up | Go back out in the sun for a while to even the colour up | Rub salt on to dry the skin | Put more sun on it so the skin gets used to it and toughens up
exercise: Your friend points a magnifying glass at a dry leaf on a sunny day. Why is that dangerous? | It focuses sunlight into a spot hot enough to start a fire | The glass may crack and break in the heat of the sun | It makes the leaf grow faster | It only hurts his eyes, and it can never do any harm to the leaf
exercise: You find a glass bottle in the dry grass of a park. What should you do? | Pick it up and put it in a bin | Leave it there, as it is not yours | Smash it on a stone so it cannot be used | Hide it deep under the dry grass
test: A girl in Sydney wants to play cricket outside at 1 p.m. in summer. What is wise? | Play in the shade or later, as the UV is strongest at midday | Play then, because the sun is at its weakest at midday | The sun in Australia is weaker than in Europe, so play | Shade does not matter if she is not feeling too hot while she plays
test: A boy in Minnesota has been in the snow for an hour and his fingers are white and numb. What should he do? | Go in, take off wet things and tell a grown-up | Rub his hands hard with handfuls of snow | Keep playing so his hands warm up on their own | Hold them close to a hot fire straight away
test: A boy's friend throws a hard, icy snowball at a passing car. What could happen? | The driver could be startled and crash | The car will just get a little bit cleaner | Nothing, because cars are made of metal | The snowball will melt long before it hits the car
test: Why is a magnifying glass in sunlight like a tiny fire starter? | It gathers sunlight into one very hot spot | It makes its own heat from inside the glass | It reflects all of the sun back up into the sky | It cools down all the air around the glass instead
test: A girl sees a broken bottle in dry summer grass. Why is it worth picking up? | Glass can focus sunlight and start a grass fire | Bottles left out in the sun grow into bigger bottles | The grass needs the glass to grow | It is only worth it if the bottle is still full of something
test: Children build a big snow tunnel next to the road after a storm. A snowplough is coming. What is the danger? | The plough could push snow onto the tunnel and bury them | The plough will melt all the snow | Snowploughs only ever clear the pavements, never the roads | The tunnel will be swept away by the wind and nothing else
test: A boy sees an older child about to knock down a huge icicle hanging over the doorway. Why should he warn him? | A falling icicle is heavy and sharp and can injure people | Icicles are needed to keep the house warm all through the winter | Icicles are too slippery to hold | It will make the roof leak at once and flood the whole house
test: A girl stays in the sun after getting sunburnt, saying it will turn into a tan. What is true? | More sun makes the burn worse | Burnt skin heals faster in sunlight | A burn protects her from more burning | Sunburn turns into a tan safely
test: A skater glides the wrong way round a busy rink. What is the risk? | Crashing into others whose sharp blades can cut | The ice will melt faster under her | The skates will break | Nothing, as long as she skates fast enough to dodge them all
test: icicles on the shed are huge, can i knock them off | No, stay away, they're heavy and sharp | Yes, with a long stick from the side | Yes, if you stand right under them | Only if they're already melting n dripping
test: thirsty, gonna eat this yellow snow | No, never eat yellow or dirty snow | Yes, snow is just clean water | Yes, yellow snow is lemon flavour lol | Only a little bit
test: been outside ages, shaking lots n feeling sleepy. normal? | No, tell a grown-up and get warm and dry | Yes, just have a little nap in the snow | Yes, keep playing it'll pass | Take off your wet gloves n carry on playing
test: got a magnifying glass, gonna burn some ants | No, never point it at anything, it can start a fire | Yes, ants don't feel it | Yes, it's fine if you do it on dry grass | Only in strong sun at midday when it works best
explain: Two boys in Minnesota walked onto a frozen pond to fetch a ball; the ice cracked and one fell through into freezing water, and firefighters had to rescue him.
explain: A girl in Canada played outside for hours in wet gloves in deep cold; her fingers turned white and numb, and the doctors treated her for frostbite.
fact: Ice that looks solid can be thin in places, and falling into freezing water makes it hard to breathe and move within minutes.
fact: If someone falls through ice, do not go out after them; shout for help and reach out to them with a long stick or branch from the bank.
fact: Frostbite can damage fingers, toes, ears and noses in the cold, and wet clothes make it happen faster.
test: ball's on the pond ice, i'll just grab it | No, leave it, ice can crack | Yes, if you're light enough | Yes, crawl out to it on ur tummy | Only near the edge
test: friend fell through the ice! | Don't go out; call for help and reach with a stick | Run out on the ice to grab him | Jump in after him and pull him out | Go home and get dry
test: Why should wet gloves be swapped for dry ones in the snow? | Wet hands freeze faster and can get frostbite | Dry gloves look much better | Wet gloves shrink in the cold until they no longer fit | Snow sticks better to dry gloves, so snowballs are easier to make
test: did u hear a kid walked on the frozen pond and fell through and couldn't breathe in the cold water? real lesson? | Ice that looks solid can be thin; stay off it | Thick-looking ice is always safe to walk on | Walk quickly so you don't fall through | Only grown-ups can fall through ice, kids are too light to break it
test: apparently a boy put a stone in a snowball and knocked out his mate's tooth. what's the true lesson? | Throw only soft snowballs, never with stones or ice | Snowballs are safe whatever you put inside | Aim only at the head for fun | Stones make snowballs fly straighter, so they're better for aiming
test: my cousin says if u eat yellow snow u die instantly lol. probably blown up? | Instantly; but don't eat yellow or dirty snow | That snow can be dirty | That yellow snow can be animal pee | That snow is frozen water
exam: Identify the signs of hypothermia. | Hard shivering with clumsiness and sleepiness | A hot face accompanied by much sweating | A sudden wish to run and play outside | Hunger and a dry mouth
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises icicles? | They are heavy and sharp, and one does not stand beneath them | They are soft and harmless when they fall | They may be knocked down safely with a stick by standing beneath them | They are safe to eat directly from roofs
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice when the UV index is high. | One remains in the shade from about 11 a.m. to 3 p.m. | One sunbathes at midday, when it is warmest | One avoids the sun only in the early hours of the morning | One requires no protection whatever if it is cloudy
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a magnifying glass is never pointed at anything in sunlight. | It focuses sunlight into a spot hot enough to start a fire | It makes objects permanently larger | It dims the sunlight so much that the eyes are harmed | It makes the glass shatter into many sharp pieces at once
